# Bracha 1987, "Asynchronous Byzantine Agreement Protocols"
# Information and Computation 75, 130-143 (1987)
#
# Figure 4 -- the consensus protocol, by process p: Figure 3's round
# three times per phase, with N given.
#
#   Phase(i): (by process p)
#   1. Broadcast(p, 3i+1, value_p).
#      Wait until validate n - t 3i+1-messages.
#      value_p := majority value of the n - t validated messages.
#   2. Broadcast(p, 3i+2, value_p).
#      Wait until validate n - t 3i+2-messages.
#      (i)  If more than n/2 of the messages have the same value v,
#           then value_p = (d, v)
#      (ii) Otherwise, value_p := value_p.
#   3. Broadcast(p, 3i+3, value_p).
#      Wait until validate n - t 3i+3-messages.
#      (i)   If validated more than 2t messages with value (d, v)
#            then decision_p := value_p := v
#      (ii)  If validated more than t messages with value (d, v)
#            then value_p := v.
#      (iii) Otherwise, value_p := coin_toss (0 or 1 with probability 1/2).
#      Go to round 1 of phase i+1
#
# From one process's seat, beyond Figure 3: value_p, and a decision.
# The round is Figure 3's; what Figure 4 adds is N at the turn and the
# witness that Figure 3 reads as one input.  Both are thresholds over
# counts of values: N over the sample the turn consumes, the witness
# over VALID^(k-1) -- and where N keeps value_p the witness reads one
# message as well, the sender's own of round k - 1, since value_p is
# what that sender broadcast there.  A round's position in its phase,
# 3i+1, 3i+2 or 3i+3, is the only thing the round number contributes.
#
# Values a message carries: 0, 1, (d, 0), (d, 1).
#
# The witness, by the round of the message being validated, is the
# figure's cases read as "could a correct process have sent this":
#   3i+2, v:       some n - t of VALID^(3i+1) have majority v, or have
#                  exactly half v and the sender's own 3i+1-message in
#                  VALID^(3i+1) carries v -- the tie reading below
#   3i+3, (d, v):  some n - t of VALID^(3i+2) have more than n/2 of v
#   3i+3, v:       some n - t of VALID^(3i+2) have no value above n/2,
#                  and the sender's own 3i+2-message in VALID^(3i+2)
#                  carries v -- step 2's "otherwise, value_p :=
#                  value_p": what is kept is what the sender broadcast
#                  at 3i+2, so a correct sender's plain v at 3i+3 is
#                  its plain v at 3i+2 and no other
#   3i+1, v:       some n - t of VALID^(3i) have more than t of (d, v),
#                  or have no (d, x) above t -- step 3's coin: any v
#   3i+1, (d, v):  never; (d, v) is sent at 3i+3 only
# Round 1 is Figure 3's own case, a value of 0 or 1.
#
# Round 1 never consults the witness: Figure 3's validate row reads it
# above round 1 only, so an empty VALID^0 rejects nothing.
#
# At the t = 0 base the sample is all n and "some n - t of VALID" is
# the whole of it.  The figure's N consumes "the n - t validated
# messages"; above t = 0 the turn consumes whatever is validated when
# it fires, at least n - t and up to n -- the reading under which the
# turn's patience buys a fuller sample: do not spend the Byzantine
# budget against a laggard.  The proofs read the same over any
# validated sample of at least n - t: the step tests are absolute
# (n/2, 2t, t), the one relative test, step 1's majority, has the
# correct messages a majority of any sample above 2t, and VALID holds
# a faulty process's message to a value a correct one could have sent.
#
# A decision does not stop the process: "Go to round 1 of phase i+1"
# is under case (i), the paper says the protocol "does not terminate
# once a decision is made", and Theorem 2's agreement needs the
# deciders' next-phase broadcasts -- Lemma 9 has every correct process
# decide in the phase after the first decision.  So a decided process
# broadcasts through the next phase, three more rounds, and then has
# nothing left to say: the "continue" row below.  Abandon remains the
# seat's only exit; a BA that stops is not an exit.
#
# FOUR READINGS THE FIGURE DOES NOT GIVE, each named by its row:
#   a tie in "majority value" (an even sample split evenly) keeps
#     value_p -- the figure names no majority then, and Ben-Or's step 2
#     treats the absence of a majority as "?": the value_p row
#     "keep,yes,3i+1,= half".  What is kept is the value the sender
#     broadcast at 3i+1, so in the witness a tie answers yes for that
#     value alone: the rows "yes,yes,3i+2,0,= half,,,,,yes" and
#     "yes,yes,3i+2,1,= half,,,,,yes", and no for the other bit.
#     Lemma 9's proof states only the necessary count -- a correct
#     process "must also validate at least (n - t)/2 messages with
#     value u" -- and stands under this reading;
#   where N keeps value_p, a validator reads the sender's value_p as the
#     sender's own validated message of the round before: Section 3
#     gives N the round number and S alone, and step 2's "value_p :=
#     value_p" reads outside S, so VALID's "v = N(k-1, S)" needs this
#     to be applied to another process's message: the witness rows
#     ending ",yes" at 3i+3 plain, and the tie rows above;
#   the coin is the caller's: the turn says "coin", the caller tosses
#     through its callback: the value_p row "coin,yes,3i+3,,,,<= t,<= t";
#   rows where both (d, 0) and (d, 1) exceed t exist for exhaustiveness
#     only: Lemma 10 makes them unreachable for a correct process, and
#     at n = 3t+1 they are impossible by arithmetic, so their outputs
#     are never taken: the value_p rows at 3i+3 with "> t" or "> 2t"
#     in both (d, 0) and (d, 1), and the decide rows likewise.
#
# Inputs (beyond Figure 3's):
#   "turn"                               { yes, no }  Figure 3's
#   "round"                              { 3i+1, 3i+2, 3i+3 }  this process's
#   "sample messages with value 1"       { > half, = half, < half }
#                                        against half the sample, 3i+1
#   "sample messages with value 0 against n/2"  { > n/2, <= n/2 }  3i+2
#   "sample messages with value 1 against n/2"  { > n/2, <= n/2 }  3i+2
#   "sample messages with (d, 0)"        { > 2t, > t, <= t }  3i+3
#   "sample messages with (d, 1)"        { > 2t, > t, <= t }  3i+3
#   "have decided"                       { yes, no }
#   "rounds since the decision"          { < 3, >= 3 }  the current round
#                                        less the deciding round
#   "message round"                      { 3i+1, 3i+2, 3i+3 }  the message
#                                        being validated
#   "message value"                      { 0, 1, (d, 0), (d, 1) }
#   "VALID^(k-1) has n-t messages"       { yes, no }
#   "VALID^(k-1) messages with the value" { > half, = half, < half }
#                                        against half of n - t, 3i+2
#   "VALID^(k-1) messages with the value against n/2"  { > n/2, <= n/2 }
#                                        3i+3, (d, v): the v of it
#   "VALID^(k-1) can be read as ?"       { yes, no }  some n - t with no
#                                        value above n/2, 3i+3
#   "VALID^(k-1) messages with (d, the value)"  { > t, <= t }  3i+1
#   "VALID^(k-1) can be read as coin"    { yes, no }  some n - t with no
#                                        (d, x) above t, 3i+1
#   "VALID^(k-1) holds the sender's (k-1)-message with the value"
#                                        { yes, no }  3i+2 on a tie and
#                                        3i+3 plain: N kept value_p,
#                                        which is what the sender
#                                        broadcast at round k - 1
#
# Outputs:
#   "value_p"      { 0, 1, (d, 0), (d, 1), keep, coin }
#   "decide"       { 0, 1, no }
#   "continue"     { yes, no }  a turn goes on to the next round
#   "n-t messages in VALID^(k-1) yield v"  { yes, no }  Figure 3's input

# ===== N at the turn =====

# Step 1: value_p := majority value of the validated messages.
@"value_p","turn","round","sample messages with value 1","sample messages with value 0 against n/2","sample messages with value 1 against n/2","sample messages with (d, 0)","sample messages with (d, 1)"
1,yes,3i+1,> half,,,,
0,yes,3i+1,< half,,,,
keep,yes,3i+1,= half,,,,
"(d, 0)",yes,3i+2,,> n/2,,,
"(d, 1)",yes,3i+2,,<= n/2,> n/2,,
keep,yes,3i+2,,<= n/2,<= n/2,,
0,yes,3i+3,,,,> 2t,
1,yes,3i+3,,,,> t,> 2t
0,yes,3i+3,,,,> t,> t
0,yes,3i+3,,,,> t,<= t
1,yes,3i+3,,,,<= t,> 2t
1,yes,3i+3,,,,<= t,> t
coin,yes,3i+3,,,,<= t,<= t
keep,no,,,,,,

# Step 3 (i): more than 2t (d, v) decides v, once.
@"decide","turn","round","have decided","sample messages with (d, 0)","sample messages with (d, 1)"
0,yes,3i+3,no,> 2t,
1,yes,3i+3,no,> t,> 2t
1,yes,3i+3,no,<= t,> 2t
no,yes,3i+3,no,> t,> t
no,yes,3i+3,no,> t,<= t
no,yes,3i+3,no,<= t,> t
no,yes,3i+3,no,<= t,<= t
no,yes,3i+3,yes,,
no,yes,3i+1,,,
no,yes,3i+2,,,
no,no,,,,

# A turn goes on to the next round while undecided, or for the phase
# after the decision; a decision made on this turn counts from now.
@"continue","turn","have decided","decide","rounds since the decision"
yes,yes,no,,
yes,yes,yes,,< 3
no,yes,yes,,>= 3
no,no,,,

# ===== the witness Figure 3 reads =====

@"n-t messages in VALID^(k-1) yield v","VALID^(k-1) has n-t messages","message round","message value","VALID^(k-1) messages with the value","VALID^(k-1) messages with the value against n/2","VALID^(k-1) can be read as ?","VALID^(k-1) messages with (d, the value)","VALID^(k-1) can be read as coin","VALID^(k-1) holds the sender's (k-1)-message with the value"
no,no,,,,,,,,
yes,yes,3i+2,0,> half,,,,,
yes,yes,3i+2,0,= half,,,,,yes
no,yes,3i+2,0,= half,,,,,no
no,yes,3i+2,0,< half,,,,,
yes,yes,3i+2,1,> half,,,,,
yes,yes,3i+2,1,= half,,,,,yes
no,yes,3i+2,1,= half,,,,,no
no,yes,3i+2,1,< half,,,,,
no,yes,3i+2,"(d, 0)",,,,,,
no,yes,3i+2,"(d, 1)",,,,,,
yes,yes,3i+3,"(d, 0)",,> n/2,,,,
no,yes,3i+3,"(d, 0)",,<= n/2,,,,
yes,yes,3i+3,"(d, 1)",,> n/2,,,,
no,yes,3i+3,"(d, 1)",,<= n/2,,,,
yes,yes,3i+3,0,,,yes,,,yes
no,yes,3i+3,0,,,yes,,,no
no,yes,3i+3,0,,,no,,,
yes,yes,3i+3,1,,,yes,,,yes
no,yes,3i+3,1,,,yes,,,no
no,yes,3i+3,1,,,no,,,
yes,yes,3i+1,0,,,,> t,,
yes,yes,3i+1,0,,,,<= t,yes,
no,yes,3i+1,0,,,,<= t,no,
yes,yes,3i+1,1,,,,> t,,
yes,yes,3i+1,1,,,,<= t,yes,
no,yes,3i+1,1,,,,<= t,no,
no,yes,3i+1,"(d, 0)",,,,,,
no,yes,3i+1,"(d, 1)",,,,,,
